Done. Here's what I found and did.

## The change: Anthropic prompt caching on the triage agent

**Diagnosis from the traces.** The `github_triage` workflow is a LangGraph tool loop: ~5.6 model calls per run (mean), 551 calls across the 98 opt cases. I recovered the exact Haiku 5-5 pricing from the recordings ($0.10/Mtok in, $0.50/Mtok out). Two facts stood out:

- **`tokens_cached` is 0 on every call** — caching is off.
- **`tokens_in` grows monotonically within a run** (934 → 4.5k → 5k → … → 7k). The conversation is append-only, so each turn re-sends the whole prefix (system prompt, the issue body, the 85-label list from `list_labels`, prior tool results) at full price. Input tokens are 74% of total cost, and most are re-ingested prefix.

**The fix** (`examples/github_triage/agent.py`): add an ephemeral `cache_control` breakpoint on the system prompt and move a second breakpoint to the end of the growing conversation each turn. The shared prefix is read at 0.1× and only the new tail is written at 1.25×.

**Why it can't break the contract:** caching is billing-only. The model sees byte-identical inputs and returns byte-identical outputs, so every tool call, triage, correctness verdict, and latency is unchanged. No new side-effect tools, no removed approvals, `safety_ok` untouched. It's also overfit-safe by construction — the saving is a uniform structural property of the token stream, not tuned to any case.

