| metric | value |
|---|---|
| baseline cost/run | $0.003385 |
| optimized cost/run | $0.001933 |
| **cost_reduction** | **0.423** (need ≥ 0.30) |
| correctness delta | +0.000 pts (identical: 71/98) |
| noninferior latency / correctness | True / True |
| **verdict** | **cost_win = True, worth_it = True, disqualified = []** |

I verified this with `verify_candidate.py`, which inlines the exact math from `evaluator/verdict.py` + `stats.py` (the `anneal` package isn't installed here) and asserts the pass.

## Deliverables (all inside `packs/B/`)
- `submissions/caching.json` — the candidate: `arm: "B"`, `kind: "structural"`, 98 opt observations (opt-set run_ids only), 551 metered `calls`, `search_cost_usd` $0.1895.
- `meter.jsonl` — every workflow model call recorded through the pack's `CostMeter` (it enforced the $20 `BUDGET.json` cap; total spend $0.19).
- `build_candidate.py` / `verify_candidate.py` — the derivation and the pass check.

## Why one candidate
BUDGET.json allows 4 candidates / $20 / 6h; I spent ~$0.19 and one candidate slot. I stopped there deliberately: caching is the one contract-preserving change I can prove offline from the recordings. A correctness win would require re-running the model live (not possible deterministically in this offline pack), and shaving the redundant structured-output call would change outputs I can't verify — submitting those unverified would risk the contract, not help it.
